(7534340, 1204509, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!  A very close relative to this image, also taken by you, took the blue in the recent Eclipse challenge - even the titles are almost identical - and honestly I have to admit that I too was feeling a bit ''eclipsed-out'' when I saw it.  Yes, it is a fabulous capture, and I appreciate all the hard work and skill that went into getting it, but perhaps too much of a good thing and the voters acted accordingly?  Sorry but I really have nowhere else to go with this.  <br/> <br/>As always, feel fee to PM me, <br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  A very close relative to this image, also taken by you, took the blue in the recent Eclipse challenge - even the titles are almost identical - and honestly I have to admit that I too was feeling a bit ''eclipsed-out'' when I saw it.  Yes, it is a fabulous capture, and I appreciate all the hard work and skill that went into getting it, but perhaps too much of a good thing and the voters acted accordingly?  Sorry but I really have nowhere else to go with this.   As always, feel fee to PM me,  Susan', '2017-09-10 20:01:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534341, 1204556, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>So we meet again, Msr Marcel...*muwahahaha...* Lighting and comp are ok in this shot but not spectacular. As did your commenters, I see the dragon-winged horse in the thumb - the ''horse'' head facing left and the tangle of roots in the upper right for wings, and the lower thirds making the body - but at full size it''s a bit tougher to see.  I agree with Irene in using a bit of dodge and burn to accentuate the primary shape, especially in b/w when it really does come down to shape and form.  The clouds in the bg help to add to the atmosphere but they may also have competed somewhat with the driftwood.  Glad to see you shot from down low, maybe next time try shooting up from even lower and see what you get. <br/> <br/>Meantime keep up the great work!<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! So we meet again, Msr Marcel...*muwahahaha...* Lighting and comp are ok in this shot but not spectacular. As did your commenters, I see the dragon-winged horse in the thumb - the ''horse'' head facing left and the tangle of roots in the upper right for wings, and the lower thirds making the body - but at full size it''s a bit tougher to see.  I agree with Irene in using a bit of dodge and burn to accentuate the primary shape, especially in b/w when it really does come down to shape and form.  The clouds in the bg help to add to the atmosphere but they may also have competed somewhat with the driftwood.  Glad to see you shot from down low, maybe next time try shooting up from even lower and see what you get.  Meantime keep up the great work! Susan', '2017-09-10 20:08:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534344, 1204509, 54446, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by snaffles:</b></div><hr/><i> Greetings from the Critique Club!  A very close relative to this image, also taken by you, took the blue in the recent Eclipse challenge - even the titles are almost identical - and honestly I have to admit that I too was feeling a bit ''eclipsed-out'' when I saw it.  Yes, it is a fabulous capture, and I appreciate all the hard work and skill that went into getting it, but perhaps too much of a good thing and the voters acted accordingly?  Sorry but I really have nowhere else to go with this.  <br/> <br/>As always, feel fee to PM me, <br/> <br/>Susan </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This image is unique.  It qualifies for entry into the August FS.  If anyone thinks this violates challenge rules, then please report the image to the administration.  Each challenge stands alone and should receive votes unrelated to other challenges.  It''s pretty much unfair to discriminate against this image because it''s a total solar eclipse photo.  In the past, images of similar subjects have been entered into consecutive challenges.  I think two or three have actually ribboned.  (remember the bridges?)</td>', 'Originally posted by snaffles: Greetings from the Critique Club!  A very close relative to this image, also taken by you, took the blue in the recent Eclipse challenge - even the titles are almost identical - and honestly I have to admit that I too was feeling a bit ''eclipsed-out'' when I saw it.  Yes, it is a fabulous capture, and I appreciate all the hard work and skill that went into getting it, but perhaps too much of a good thing and the voters acted accordingly?  Sorry but I really have nowhere else to go with this.   As always, feel fee to PM me,  Susan   This image is unique.  It qualifies for entry into the August FS.  If anyone thinks this violates challenge rules, then please report the image to the administration.  Each challenge stands alone and should receive votes unrelated to other challenges.  It''s pretty much unfair to discriminate against this image because it''s a total solar eclipse photo.  In the past, images of similar subjects have been entered into consecutive challenges.  I think two or three have actually ribboned.  (remember the bridges?)', '2017-09-10 20:53:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534460, 1204857, 113591, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by mitalapo:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by mitalapo:</b></div><hr/><i> just make sure not to tell what this is. please. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>unless it is the potato dancer, that is. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>With a clear conscience, without fear of repudiation, I can state that this may or may not be the potato dancer.<br/> <br/>The fact that I do not know anything called the paotato dancer may have something to do with it.<br/> <br/>It really is a shot at my sister''s house taken from west to east, at about hip level from a distance of 3 metres, of a mmmffffff....<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by mitalapo: Originally posted by mitalapo: just make sure not to tell what this is. please.  unless it is the potato dancer, that is.   With a clear conscience, without fear of repudiation, I can state that this may or may not be the potato dancer. The fact that I do not know anything called the paotato dancer may have something to do with it. It really is a shot at my sister''s house taken from west to east, at about hip level from a distance of 3 metres, of a mmmffffff....', '2017-09-11 03:24:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534475, 1204905, 25024, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club<br/> <br/>An appealing image that contributes to the open challenge<br/> <br/>What an interesting mix of colourful birds you have captured Susan. The bright red bird on the right is particularly engaging which is in contrast to the other two birds whose position and focus I feel is somewhat marring the end result to the detriment of that gorgeous bird. Thank you for your notes because this helps to see the image in the way you saw it but unfortunately your voters would not have had that same luxury</td>', 'Hello from the critique club An appealing image that contributes to the open challenge What an interesting mix of colourful birds you have captured Susan. The bright red bird on the right is particularly engaging which is in contrast to the other two birds whose position and focus I feel is somewhat marring the end result to the detriment of that gorgeous bird. Thank you for your notes because this helps to see the image in the way you saw it but unfortunately your voters would not have had that same luxury', '2017-09-11 07:11:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534478, 1204832, 25024, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club<br/> <br/>An appealing image that contributes well to the open challenge<br/> <br/>A great viewpoint that makes for an interesting and dynamic scene. My first reaction was not too positive feeling that it was over-processed and that is somewhat still my feeling, however, the more I look at it the more it grows on me. I am enjoying the immediate foreground seas the most, in fact, if I crop it just below the upper background structure I feel it makes it a far stronger image, Id be interested in your thoughts?</td>', 'Hello from the critique club An appealing image that contributes well to the open challenge A great viewpoint that makes for an interesting and dynamic scene. My first reaction was not too positive feeling that it was over-processed and that is somewhat still my feeling, however, the more I look at it the more it grows on me. I am enjoying the immediate foreground seas the most, in fact, if I crop it just below the upper background structure I feel it makes it a far stronger image, Id be interested in your thoughts?', '2017-09-11 07:27:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534479, 1204796, 25024, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club<br/> <br/>An appealing image that contributes well to the open challenge<br/> <br/>What a fascinating scene, well seen and captured Joanna. Technically, I think your focus could have been improved bringing it closer to capture their feelers sharper as opposed to the leaf itself, this would also have rendered the eggs a little softer but still clearly identifiable for what they are. Its still a great shot, well done Joanna.</td>', 'Hello from the critique club An appealing image that contributes well to the open challenge What a fascinating scene, well seen and captured Joanna. Technically, I think your focus could have been improved bringing it closer to capture their feelers sharper as opposed to the leaf itself, this would also have rendered the eggs a little softer but still clearly identifiable for what they are. Its still a great shot, well done Joanna.', '2017-09-11 07:32:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534480, 1204776, 25024, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club<br/> <br/>An unusual image that contributes to the open challenge<br/> <br/>Welcome to DPC! Your entry is commendable for its uniqueness but their are some technical problems with it. The lighting is too severe causing dense hard shadows that obscure what is probably interesting detail on the board. I think you probably missed a great opportunity to take a very different image from eye level with the die excluding the left where the dark shadows are concentrating more on the shapes and translucent effects the lighting has on the dice themselves?</td>', 'Hello from the critique club An unusual image that contributes to the open challenge Welcome to DPC! Your entry is commendable for its uniqueness but their are some technical problems with it. The lighting is too severe causing dense hard shadows that obscure what is probably interesting detail on the board. I think you probably missed a great opportunity to take a very different image from eye level with the die excluding the left where the dark shadows are concentrating more on the shapes and translucent effects the lighting has on the dice themselves?', '2017-09-11 07:39:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(7534481, 1204506, 25024, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club<br/> <br/>An unusual image that contributes to the open challenge<br/> <br/>An interesting idea that works to a degree. Whilst I would normally prefer mono, I suspect that this a very colourful character? There are two very distracting elements that spoil it for me, the main one being the background character that interferes with the main character in such a way that it detracts from what would have been a very strong figure. The other is the crop, the figure at the top should have been removed completely, again it detracts</td>', 'Hello from the critique club An unusual image that contributes to the open challenge An interesting idea that works to a degree. Whilst I would normally prefer mono, I suspect that this a very colourful character? There are two very distracting elements that spoil it for me, the main one being the background character that interferes with the main character in such a way that it detracts from what would have been a very strong figure. The other is the crop, the figure at the top should have been removed completely, again it detracts', '2017-09-11 07:45:55.000000', NULL, 0),
